<header class="ref-head">
  <span class="hero-ornament"></span>
  <h1 class="research-title">Forms of Government</h1>
<!-- build:gov-lede -->
<p class="ref-lede">How 20 forms of government have worked, from the chiefdoms of the first farming peoples to the parliaments and federations of today: who held power in each, how rulers were chosen, and the case made for and against them.</p>
<!-- /build:gov-lede -->
</header>

<section class="gv-six" aria-labelledby="gv-six-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="gv-six-t">Who should rule? Aristotle&#8217;s six forms</h2>
  <p class="gv-p">Aristotle&#8217;s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politics-aristotle">Politics</a>, written in the 4th century BCE with the help of a collection of 158 constitutions made in his school, asked two questions of every government: how many rule, and for whose good. One, a few or the many may rule; they may rule for the good of all or for their own. The answers gave six forms, three true and three corrupt, and the scheme has framed the comparison of governments ever since.</p>
  <div class="gv-grid6" role="table" aria-label="Aristotle's six forms of government">
    <div class="gv-g6-row gv-g6-h" role="row"><span role="columnheader"></span><span role="columnheader">For the common good</span><span role="columnheader">For the rulers&#8217; own good</span></div>
    <div class="gv-g6-row" role="row"><span class="gv-g6-k" role="rowheader">Rule by one</span><span class="gv-g6-c gv-good" role="cell"><b>Kingship</b><i>basileia</i><small>see the three <a href="#gv-one">monarchies</a></small></span><span class="gv-g6-c gv-bad" role="cell"><b><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny</a></b><i>tyrannis</i></span></div>
    <div class="gv-g6-row" role="row"><span class="gv-g6-k" role="rowheader">Rule by a few</span><span class="gv-g6-c gv-good" role="cell"><b><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy</a></b><i>aristokratia</i></span><span class="gv-g6-c gv-bad" role="cell"><b><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy</a></b><i>oligarchia</i></span></div>
    <div class="gv-g6-row" role="row"><span class="gv-g6-k" role="rowheader">Rule by the many</span><span class="gv-g6-c gv-good" role="cell"><b>Polity</b><i>politeia</i><small>the mixed constitution: see the <a href="/government/republic">Republic</a></small></span><span class="gv-g6-c gv-bad" role="cell"><b><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Democracy</a></b><i>demokratia</i></span></div>
  </div>
  <p class="gv-p">Aristotle counted democracy among the corrupt forms because he understood it as rule by the poor in their own interest; he judged the best constitution within reach a polity, mixing oligarchy and democracy and resting on citizens of middling wealth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, a Greek writing in Rome in the 2nd century BCE, turned the six into a cycle in which each good form decays into its corruption and gives way to the next, and found Rome&#8217;s stability in a constitution that mixed all three. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, in 1748, sorted governments differently, into republics, monarchies and despotisms, each with its own principle: virtue, honour and fear.</p>
</section>

<section class="gv-design" id="design" aria-labelledby="gv-design-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="gv-design-t">Design your state</h2>
  <p class="gv-p">Seven questions about who should rule, how they are chosen and what holds them back, and the form of government your answers most resemble.</p>
  <div class="gv-wiz" id="gv-wiz" aria-live="polite"><noscript><p class="pass-note">The questions need JavaScript; the twenty forms are listed below.</p></noscript></div>
</section>

<section class="gv-forms" aria-labelledby="gv-forms-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="gv-forms-t">Twenty forms of government</h2>
  <p class="gv-p">Each form has a profile of its own: how it works, where it began, its course through history, the case made for it and against it, and its legacy.</p>
<!-- build:gov-forms -->
<section class="gv-group" id="gv-one" aria-labelledby="gv-one-t"><h3 class="gv-group-t" id="gv-one-t">Rule by one <span class="n">5</span></h3><p class="gv-group-d">A single ruler holds supreme power, by inheritance, election or force.</p><ul class="gv-cards"><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/absolute-monarchy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Absolute monarchy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Absolute monarchy</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 1500 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Rule by a hereditary monarch who holds supreme power unchecked by any constitution, parliament or court: the form claimed by the great monarchies of early modern Europe and kept by a handful of states today.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/constitutional-monarchy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Constitutional monarchy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Constitutional monarchy</span><span class="gv-card-y">1689 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">A monarchy in which the sovereign reigns under a constitution while elected parliaments and ministers govern: the form of most surviving monarchies, from the United Kingdom and Spain to Japan and Thailand.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/elective-monarchy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Elective monarchy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Elective monarchy</span><span class="gv-card-y">633 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">A monarchy in which the ruler is chosen by election rather than by birth, by princes, nobles, councils or fellow rulers: the form of the Holy Roman Empire and Poland–Lithuania, and of the papacy and Malaysia today.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/tyranny"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Tyranny.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Tyranny</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 657 BCE – c. 146 BCE</span><span class="gv-card-d">One-man rule seized outside the traditional order of a Greek city-state, common from the 7th century BCE, whose name Plato and Aristotle turned into the West’s word for cruel and lawless power.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/dictatorship"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Dictatorship.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Dictatorship</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 501 BCE – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Rule by one leader or a small group holding near-absolute power, unchecked by law or free elections: named after the Roman Republic’s emergency magistrate, it became the modern name for unaccountable rule.</span></span></a></li></ul></section><section class="gv-group" id="gv-few" aria-labelledby="gv-few-t"><h3 class="gv-group-t" id="gv-few-t">Rule by a few <span class="n">5</span></h3><p class="gv-group-d">A small group governs: the well-born, the rich, the clergy, the officers or a single party.</p><ul class="gv-cards"><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/aristocracy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Aristocracy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Aristocracy</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 750 BCE – c. 1918</span><span class="gv-card-d">Rule by a small privileged class claiming to be the best fitted to govern, in practice usually a hereditary nobility, from the early Greek city-states to the upper houses of Europe’s monarchies.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/oligarchy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Oligarchy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Oligarchy</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 700 BCE – 1797</span><span class="gv-card-d">Rule by a few, usually the rich or well-born, in their own interest: the constitution of many Greek cities and of merchant republics such as Venice, and today a loose term for the political power of great wealth.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/theocracy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Theocracy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Theocracy</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 515 BCE – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Government in the name of God or the gods, by priests, a sacred ruler or religious law; the word was coined for ancient Judea, and the Papal States, Tibet under the Dalai Lamas and Iran are classic examples.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/military-junta"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Military junta.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Military junta</span><span class="gv-card-y">1170 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Government by a council of military officers, usually installed by a coup and ruling by decree in the name of order or national salvation; common after 1945, above all in Latin America, Africa and Asia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/one-party-state"><img src="/logos/thumbs/One-party state.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">One-party state</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 1918 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">A state in which one political party holds a legal or practical monopoly of power and others are banned or kept subordinate: the form of communist, fascist and many post-colonial regimes since 1918.</span></span></a></li></ul></section><section class="gv-group" id="gv-many" aria-labelledby="gv-many-t"><h3 class="gv-group-t" id="gv-many-t">Rule by the many <span class="n">4</span></h3><p class="gv-group-d">The citizens govern themselves, in person or through those they elect.</p><ul class="gv-cards"><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/direct-democracy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Direct democracy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Direct democracy</span><span class="gv-card-y">508 BCE – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Government in which the citizens themselves decide laws and policies, in an open assembly or by popular vote, instead of through elected representatives: practised in classical Athens and in Switzerland today.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/republic"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Republic.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Republic</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 509 BCE – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Government without a monarch, in which the state is the public’s affair, run by elected magistrates and councils under law: born in ancient Rome, revived in medieval Italy and the Netherlands, now the usual form.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/parliamentary-democracy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Parliamentary democracy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Parliamentary democracy</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 1721 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Democracy in which the government is formed from an elected parliament and stays in office only while it keeps parliament’s confidence: born in 18th-century Britain, now the usual form in Europe and the Commonwealth.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/presidential-democracy"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Presidential democracy.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Presidential democracy</span><span class="gv-card-y">1789 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Democracy in which a separately elected president heads the government for a fixed term and cannot be dismissed by the legislature, under a constitution that divides and balances powers: devised in the United States.</span></span></a></li></ul></section><section class="gv-group" id="gv-other" aria-labelledby="gv-other-t"><h3 class="gv-group-t" id="gv-other-t">Other ways of ordering a state <span class="n">6</span></h3><p class="gv-group-d">Forms defined by how power is divided, shared out or held over others.</p><ul class="gv-cards"><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/chiefdom"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Chiefdom.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Chiefdom</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 5000 BCE – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">A chiefdom unites several villages under a hereditary paramount chief who rules through kinship, ritual and tribute rather than officials, a form that governed peoples from ancient Mesopotamia to Hawaii and Africa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/nomadic-confederation"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Nomadic confederation.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Nomadic confederation</span><span class="gv-card-y">209 BCE – 1757</span><span class="gv-card-d">A nomadic confederation joins herding tribes under one ruling clan and its khan; from the Xiongnu to the Mongols such steppe powers built the largest land empires and drew tribute from China and Constantinople.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/city-state"><img src="/logos/thumbs/City-state.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">City-state</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 3500 BCE – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">A city-state is a single city that governs itself and its surrounding countryside; from Sumer, Greece and Phoenicia to Venice and the Hanseatic towns it nurtured citizenship, trade and republican government.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/feudalism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Feudalism.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Feudalism</span><span class="gv-card-y">c. 800 – 1789</span><span class="gv-card-d">Feudalism was the medieval European order in which lords granted fiefs to vassals for military service and loyalty, leaving public power divided among landholders; historians now debate whether the term fits.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/federation"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Federation.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Federation</span><span class="gv-card-y">1789 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">A state whose constitution divides power between a central government and self-governing member states, neither able to abolish the other: pioneered by the United States in 1789, now home to some 40 per cent of humanity.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="gv-card" href="/government/colonial-rule"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Colonial rule.png" alt="" width="52" height="52" loading="lazy"><span class="gv-card-b"><span class="gv-card-t">Colonial rule</span><span class="gv-card-y">1415 – today</span><span class="gv-card-d">Colonial rule is government of a territory and its people by a distant state that treats them as subjects rather than citizens; from 1415 to the 20th century it carried European power across most of the world.</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<!-- /build:gov-forms -->
</section>

