<section class="gv-compare" aria-labelledby="gv-compare-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="gv-compare-t">The forms compared</h2>
  <p class="gv-p">Who holds power in each form, where its right to rule comes from, and how its rulers are chosen, as the profiles set them out.</p>
<!-- build:gov-table -->
<div class="gv-tablewrap"><table class="gv-cmp"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Form</th><th scope="col">Power held by</th><th scope="col">Legitimacy from</th><th scope="col">How rulers are chosen</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="gv-cmp-g"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="4">Rule by one</th></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy</a></th><td>The monarch alone, as supreme lawgiver, judge and commander, governing through ministers, councils and officials who serve at the ruler’s pleasure</td><td>Hereditary right sanctified by religion: in early modern Europe the divine right of kings, by which the ruler answered to God alone; elsewhere such sanctions as the Mandate of Heaven</td><td>By hereditary succession within a dynasty, usually by primogeniture; a monarch left the throne only by death, abdication, deposition or revolution</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy</a></th><td>Shared under a constitution: in most, an elected parliament and a cabinet answerable to it govern while the monarch acts on ministers’ advice; in some the monarch keeps real executive powers</td><td>Hereditary right bound by law: the crown’s authority rests on a constitution, statutes and conventions, the government’s on the consent of voters expressed through parliament</td><td>The monarch by hereditary succession regulated by law, such as the Act of Settlement 1701; ministers through parliamentary elections and the confidence of the legislature</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy</a></th><td>The elected monarch, usually for life, often bound by conditions the electors imposed as the price of the crown, such as electoral capitulations or the Polish pacta conventa</td><td>Choice by a recognised body of electors, usually from a royal house or a qualified class, sealed by oath, coronation or religious consecration</td><td>Elected on the ruler’s death or abdication, sometimes in his lifetime, by prince-electors, the assembled nobility, a council of rulers or cardinals; in Malaysia for a five-year term</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny</a></th><td>A single ruler, the tyrannos, backed by a bodyguard or mercenaries, with relatives and allies in the main offices and the city’s laws and magistracies often left in place</td><td>None by law or ancestral right: power won by force, fraud or popular support against the ruling aristocrats, and kept by results, patronage, religious display and fear</td><td>Seized by coup, often with popular or mercenary backing; usually passed to sons but seldom beyond them; ended by assassination, revolt or the intervention of Sparta and other powers</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship</a></th><td>A dictator, or in military and one-party regimes a junta or party leadership, ruling through an inner circle of officers, officials, relatives and allies; legislatures and courts are subordinate</td><td>Claimed from emergency, revolution, national salvation or ideology, often dressed in constitutions and uncompetitive elections; in Rome, a lawful appointment for a single task</td><td>Power taken by coup, revolution, foreign backing or an elected leader’s self-coup; lost through death, coup, revolt, defeat or negotiated transition; in Rome named by a consul</td></tr></tbody><tbody><tr class="gv-cmp-g"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="4">Rule by a few</th></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy</a></th><td>A small hereditary or landed elite, acting through councils, senates, diets and upper houses, often beside a monarch who depended on it</td><td>A claim to excellence of birth, virtue and education, upheld by landed wealth, military service, custom and royal grants of title</td><td>Membership passes by birth and is guarded by intermarriage; monarchs ennoble newcomers, and councils co-opt or elect their members from the privileged families</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy</a></th><td>A small group defined by wealth or birth, which alone holds the councils, magistracies and votes</td><td>Property, birth or entry in a closed register, defended as the stake and competence of those with most to lose; the laws were made by and for the few</td><td>Members elect or co-opt one another into councils and short-term offices; property qualifications or hereditary lists such as Venice’s Golden Book keep others out</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy</a></th><td>A religious authority, whether a priesthood, a supreme cleric or a ruler held to be divinely guided, with the final say over law and government</td><td>The claim that sovereignty belongs to God or the gods, whose will is known through scripture, revelation, sacred law or an inspired leader</td><td>Usually by religious bodies rather than voters: popes are elected by cardinals, Dalai Lamas are found as reincarnations, and Iran’s Supreme Leader is chosen by an assembly of jurists</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta</a></th><td>A committee of senior officers, usually the commanders of the army, navy and air force and sometimes the police, ruling by decree</td><td>A claim to rescue the nation from disorder, corruption or subversion, resting on control of the armed forces and often on a promise of later elections</td><td>Officers take power by coup and fill the council by rank; members change through internal manoeuvres or counter-coups, and juntas end by handing over to civilians or being overthrown</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state</a></th><td>The ruling party’s leadership, usually a politburo or central committee and the party leader, whose decisions the organs of the state carry out</td><td>An official ideology or national mission, such as building socialism, national rebirth or independence and development, which the party claims alone to embody</td><td>Leaders rise through the party hierarchy and are chosen by its congresses and committees; elections, where held, offer only candidates the party approves</td></tr></tbody><tbody><tr class="gv-cmp-g"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="4">Rule by the many</th></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy</a></th><td>The citizens themselves, meeting in an assembly or voting in referendums and initiatives; councils and officials prepare and carry out their decisions</td><td>Popular sovereignty: laws bind because the citizens who must obey them have voted on them, each with an equal vote</td><td>Officials are drawn by lot or elected for short terms and answer to the assembly or the voters, who may audit, recall or exile them</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/republic">Republic</a></th><td>Elected magistrates, councils and assemblies who hold office for limited terms under the law; there is no hereditary monarch</td><td>The state as res publica, the “public thing” of its citizens governed under law, not the property of a ruling family</td><td>Officials are elected for fixed terms, often in colleges of two or more; in older republics only a narrow body of patricians, nobles or merchants could vote or stand</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy</a></th><td>A prime minister and cabinet who command a majority in the elected lower house; a monarch or president serves as a largely ceremonial head of state</td><td>The election of the parliament by the voters, and the continuing confidence of its majority in the government</td><td>The head of state appoints whoever can command the confidence of parliament, usually the leader of the largest party or coalition; a vote of no confidence or a lost election removes the government</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy</a></th><td>An elected president who is both head of state and head of government, checked by a separately elected legislature and an independent judiciary</td><td>A written constitution and the separate popular mandates of the president and the legislature</td><td>The president is elected by the people, directly or through an electoral college, for a fixed term, often with a limit on re-election; removal only by impeachment or for incapacity</td></tr></tbody><tbody><tr class="gv-cmp-g"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="4">Other ways of ordering a state</th></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom</a></th><td>A paramount chief from the senior line of a ranked descent group, above lesser chiefs who ruled villages or districts and passed tribute upward</td><td>Descent from founding ancestors or gods, rites that only chiefs could perform, success in war, and generosity in feasts and gifts</td><td>Inherited within the chiefly lineage, usually by seniority of descent, but often contested among close kin and settled by councils or by war</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation</a></th><td>A supreme ruler from one ruling clan (the Xiongnu chanyu, a khagan or khan), with princes, tribal chiefs and appointed commanders governing the wings and units of the army</td><td>A mandate from Heaven (Tengri) granted to the ruling clan, proved by victory and by the plunder, tribute and trade the ruler shared with his followers</td><td>Raised by an assembly of the ruling family and chiefs, the Mongol kurultai, from among the founder’s descendants, often after a contest among kinsmen decided by war</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/city-state">City-state</a></th><td>The citizens or leading families of one city and its farmland, governed by a king, a council, elected magistrates or an assembly under the city’s own laws</td><td>The city’s gods, founders and laws, and the loyalty of citizens who defended it and shared in its government</td><td>Varied: hereditary kings in Sumer and Phoenicia, election or lot among citizens in Greek poleis, councils and elected magistrates in Italian communes and Hanseatic towns</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism</a></th><td>A king or prince at the head of a hierarchy of lords and vassals, each lord exercising justice and command over the land and people granted to him</td><td>Oaths of homage and fealty, the grant of the fief, custom, and the Church’s teaching that society was ordered into those who pray, those who fight and those who work</td><td>Kingdoms and fiefs passed by inheritance; each heir did homage to his lord and paid a relief before taking possession, and a disloyal vassal could forfeit his fief</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/federation">Federation</a></th><td>Divided by the constitution between a federal government and the governments of the member states, each supreme in its own fields</td><td>A written constitution, usually a compact of the member states or their peoples, that neither level of government can change alone</td><td>Each level has its own elected government; the member states are usually represented in an upper house of the federal parliament, such as a senate or federal council</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule</a></th><td>A distant metropolitan state, crown or chartered company, acting through appointed governors, viceroys and officials, and under indirect rule through local rulers it supervised</td><td>Conquest, treaties made under pressure, papal grants and claims of discovery, justified by a Christian or “civilising” mission and by beliefs in racial superiority</td><td>Governors and viceroys were appointed by the metropole; the colonised had little or no say until elected councils, nationalist movements and independence won them representation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<!-- /build:gov-table -->
</section>

<section class="gv-how" aria-labelledby="gv-how-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="gv-how-t">How these profiles were written</h2>
  <p class="gv-p">Each profile was drafted from the standard histories and works of political science named in its references, then checked claim by claim; strengths and weaknesses are given as the arguments of named writers and scholars, with the historical evidence each side cites, and present-day states are described only as matters of fact. A form&#8217;s dates are those of the first clear example the profile treats as its beginning, and its last where it is no longer practised. The chart counts the states of the Institute&#8217;s <a href="/state/">register</a> by the kind each is recorded as: a register of states, not of constitutions, so a kingdom that became a republic usually appears as two states. The register is free to download as <a href="/dataset">open data</a>.</p>
